  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nCold air continues to slowly filter in over the Great Lakes and\nwith the flow more northwesterly, some lake effect rain showers\nlikely develop. Day time temperatures will be around \nclimatological averages for mid november (50's) though cloud \ncover at night will keep lows a little warmer than average\n(30's-40). Additional rainfall amounts with the lake effect \nshowers today will be mostly light, with less than a tenth of an\ninch expected for most of the area. NEPA may miss out on most \nof the precipitation tonight into tomorrow. \n\nAn additional shortwave dives out of the NW on the back side of\nthe exiting shortwave trough that brings a reinforcing shot of \ncooler air. 850 mb temps drop back to just below 0C allowing for\nmore lake effect rain showers to develop. The flow may allow\nthese to continue into Wednesday as the rest of the region dries\nout a bit. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nNot much overall change in the overall setup for later this\nweek. A clipper type system looks to track into the Ohio Valley\nWednesday and Thursday. This feature then potentially \ntranslates to a miller-b coastal off of the Mid- Atlantic by\nFriday. The 00Z operational ECMWF and GFS runs (11/8) represent\nthe northern end of the ensemble spread with some other member \ntracks well south of the region. Uncertainity remains high with \nthe details but continue to feature chances of rain and snow \nThursday and Friday with some rain and snow showers lingering \ninto Saturday on the backside of the low. For measurable snow, \nhigher elevations are still favored if the low track is just \nsouth of the region. This would allow enough cold air/dynamical \ncooling to occur with precipitation.",
